 {"class_id": 3079, "class": "go-bwap-backward-search-affine-gap-band-pruning", "title": "go-bwap-backward-search-affine-gap-band-pruning", "description": "go-bwap-backward-search-affine-gap-band-pruning", "answer_id": 4814, "language": "go", "environment": "go1.26", "version": "1.26", "solution": "The complete, verified solution is written to `~/bwa-fmindex/SOLUTION.md` (full source + tests included). Here is the solution document:\n\n---\n\n# Verified Solution: BWA-style FM-index backward search + adaptive affine-gap banded alignment\n\n**Problem class:** `go-bwap-backward-search-affine-gap-band-pruning`\n**Toolchain:** Go 1.26 (`go1.26.8 linux/amd64`)\n\nThe module lives at `~/bwa-fmindex` and implements an exact FM-index backward search (wavelet-matrix rank + Occ/SA checkpoints), affine-gap Smith-Waterman with a seed-derived adaptive diagonal band, full CIGAR traceback, and a saturating MAPQ.\n\n## Root-cause analysis\n\nNaive implementations fail in five concrete ways; all five were encountered and fixed while building this solution.\n\n**RC0 — SA prefix-doubling rank tie-break used the wrong offset.** Sorting uses `(rank[i], rank[i+k])`, but the rank-assignment step compared `rank[a+1]` instead of `rank[a+k]`. It is correct only while `k==1`; afterwards the FM-index silently corrupts (`Count` returns 0 for present patterns, `Locate` returns unrelated positions).\n```go\n// before: if a+1 < n { ra = rnk[a+1] }\nif a+kk < n { ra = rnk[a+kk] }   // after\n```\n\n**RC1 — affine gap-extension state was never stored.** `e`/`f` were computed and used in `H` but never written to the reused buffers, so every `E[i-1][j]-GapExtend` / `F[i][j-1]-GapExtend` read stale/zero data and gaps longer than 1 were effectively free.\n```go\nHcur[j] = best\nEcur[j] = e   // <-- required\nFcur[j] = f   // <-- required\n```\n\n**RC2 — first-row buffer leak.** The `E` recurrence was skipped on row 0 (`if !first`), so `Eprev` for row 1 was the zero-initialised buffer instead of `-inf`, making a length-1 gap cost `-GapExtend` rather than `-GapOpen`. Fix: always evaluate `E` (border `H[-1][*]=0`, `E[-1][*]=-inf` is already encoded).\n\n**RC3 — band pruning can clip the optimum.** With no seed the band collapses around diagonal 0; a spurious/repetitive seed picks a wrong diagonal. Fix without widening: use the complete matrix when no seed is found, and after a banded pass detect a traceback that touched a band edge and re-run the exact unbanded matrix. Thus `Widen == 0` can never lose the optimum.\n\n**RC4 — MAPQ must saturate on repeats.** A linear uniqueness term stays high for thousands of hits. Use a multiplicative repeat penalty:\n```\nuniq = 1 - second/best;  pen = 1/occ;  mapq = round(60 * uniq * pen)\n```\n\n## Exact fix\n\nThe critical edits are shown above. Full verified sources were generated at `~/bwa-fmindex`:\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `fm/wavelet.go` | wavelet-matrix rank with 64-bit Occ checkpoints |\n| `fm/index.go` | sentinel BWT, `C[]`, backward search, SA checkpoints + LF `Locate` |\n| `align/align.go` | banded affine SW, traceback, clipping detection |\n| `align/reference.go` | independent unbanded full-matrix reference |\n| `mapper/mapper.go` | seeding, adaptive band, exact fallback |\n| `mapper/mapq.go` | saturating MAPQ |\n\nKey mapper logic:\n```go\nbanded := have\nvar bandFor align.BandFn\nif banded {\n    bandFor = func(i int) (int, int) { c := i + d0; return c - half, c + half }\n} else {\n    bandFor = align.FullBand(m, refLen)   // no seed -> exact\n}\nbest, cigar, refStart, clipped := align.AlignTrace(read, refOf(idx), p.Scoring, bandFor)\nif banded && clipped {                    // edge hit -> exact fallback, never widen\n    best, cigar, refStart, _ = align.AlignTrace(read, refOf(idx), p.Scoring, align.FullBand(m, refLen))\n    res.Fallback = true\n    banded = false\n}\n```\n\nThe exact full code for every file is in `SOLUTION.md`.\n\n## Verification\n\n```bash\ncd ~/bwa-fmindex\ngofmt -l .        # prints nothing\ngo vet ./...      # prints nothing\ngo test ./... -count=1 -v\n```\n\nObserved (Go 1.26.8, linux/amd64):\n\n```\n--- PASS: TestFullBandMatchesReference (0.07s)\n--- PASS: TestCigarScoreConsistency (0.02s)\n--- PASS: TestFullBandMatchesReferenceWithIndels (1.84s)\n--- PASS: TestBandedContainingOptimumMatchesFull (0.12s)\nok      example.com/bwafm/align     2.056s\n--- PASS: TestBackwardSearchMatchesNaive (0.02s)\n--- PASS: TestLocateMatchesNaive (0.00s)\n--- PASS: TestWaveletRank (0.00s)\nok      example.com/bwafm/fm        0.032s\n--- PASS: TestBandedMatchesUnbandedAndSpeed (13.57s)\n--- PASS: TestZeroWideningNeverLosesOptimal (2.15s)\n--- PASS: TestMapQSaturatesOnRepetitiveSeeds (0.00s)\n--- PASS: TestRepetitiveReferenceGivesLowMapQ (0.01s)\nok      example.com/bwafm/mapper    15.731s\n```\n\nHeadline test output: `banded=282ms unbanded=6.355s speedup=22.5x cells ratio=40.3x fallbacks=15/10000`.\n\n| Requirement | Test | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| FM-index exact | `TestBackwardSearchMatchesNaive`, `TestLocateMatchesNaive` | 8 000 random patterns match naive scan |\n| Wavelet rank exact | `TestWaveletRank` | 6 000 random rank queries + `At` over every symbol |\n| Banded score == unbanded on 10 000 reads | `TestBandedMatchesUnbandedAndSpeed` | all 10 000 scores equal independent `ReferenceScore` |\n| Banded path ≥5× faster | same test | wall-clock ~14–22×, DP cells ~40× |\n| Zero-width widening never loses optimum | `TestZeroWideningNeverLosesOptimal` | `Widen=0`, 3 000 reads, all optimal; boundary-clipped paths fall back to exact |\n| MAPQ saturates on repeats | `TestMapQSaturatesOnRepetitiveSeeds`, `TestRepetitiveReferenceGivesLowMapQ` | monotone non-increasing, →0 at high support, low on a 50× repeat |\n\nThe full document with every source file and test file embedded is available at `~/bwa-fmindex/SOLUTION.md`.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: go-bwap-backward-search-affine-gap-band-pruning\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-31T05:57:33.936Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Implement a Burrows-Wheeler Aligner-style exact backward search over a suffix-array FM-index (Occ/SA checkpoints, wavelet-matrix rank) combined with affine-gap Smith-Waterman banded alignment whose band is adaptively widened by a seed-hit diagonal test. Traceback must report CIGAR with correct soft-clip/M/=/X/I/D ops plus a calibrated MAPQ that saturates on repetitive seeds. Include unit tests proving identical alignment scores to an unbanded reference on 10k random reads while the banded path is at least 5x faster, and that zero-width band widening never loses the optimal score.", "environment": "go1.26", "language": "go", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "go-bwap-backward-search-affine-gap-band-pruning", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-31T05:57:33.944Z", "version": "1.26"}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-31 05:57:33"}
 {"class_id": 3080, "class": "python3-online-softmax-flash-attention-backward-recompute", "title": "python3-online-softmax-flash-attention-backward-recompute", "description": "python3-online-softmax-flash-attention-backward-recompute", "answer_id": 4815, "language": "python3", "environment": "python3", "version": "3.12", "solution": "Implemented and verified. Full markdown write-up is saved at `~/SOLUTION.md`, with code in `~/flash_attn.py` and tests in `~/test_flash_attn.py`.\n\n# Fused (Flash-Attention) Online-Softmax Attention with LSE-Based Backward\n\n## Root-cause analysis\n\n1. **`0/0` and `-inf - -inf` on fully-masked rows.** A row with every key masked gives `rowmax=-inf`, `l=0`, `L=-inf`. `exp(S-m)`, `exp(S-L)` and `acc/l` all produce `NaN`, which then contaminates gradients. Fix: guard divisions (`where(l>0,...,1)`, zero result) and use **masked ufuncs** (`np.subtract(..., out=..., where=...)` then `np.exp(..., out=..., where=...)`). `np.where` alone still evaluates the NaN branch.\n2. **Dropping the online-softmax correction.** When a tile raises `m_old → m_new`, both `l` and `acc` must be rescaled by `alpha = exp(m_old - m_new)` before adding the tile. Otherwise output is just the last tile's softmax.\n3. **Storing probabilities in backward.** Requirement is to save only `L` (shape `(B,H,N)`) and recompute `P = exp(S-L)` per tile, keeping `O(N)` rather than `O(N·M)` memory.\n4. **Finite negative masking constant.** `-1e9` leaks softmax mass when logits are very negative. Use true `-inf`.\n5. **Ragged final tiles.** Clamp `i1=min(i0+bq,N)` and build masks on the actual slice; never assume divisibility.\n6. **fp16 accumulation.** Inputs are fp16, but `m`, `l`, `acc`, rescale, and `P@V` must accumulate in fp32.\n7. **Wrong `dS` correction.** It is `dS = P*(dP - D)` with `D_i = Σ_j dO_ij·O_ij`, not a mismatched-broadcast surrogate.\n\n## Exact fix\n\nThe implementation (key parts):\n\n```python\n# forward: online softmax, fp32 accumulation, masked ufuncs\nm_new = np.maximum(m, np.max(S, axis=-1))\nrow_ok = np.isfinite(m_new)\nalpha = np.zeros_like(m)\nrescale = row_ok & np.isfinite(m)\nnp.subtract(m, m_new, out=alpha, where=rescale)\nnp.exp(alpha, out=alpha, where=rescale)\n\nP = np.zeros_like(S)\np_mask = keep & row_ok[..., None]\nnp.subtract(S, m_new[..., None], out=P, where=p_mask)\nnp.exp(P, out=P, where=p_mask)\n\nl = l * alpha + P.sum(axis=-1)\nacc = acc * alpha[..., None] + np.einsum(\"bhnm,bhmd->bhnd\", P, Vj)\nm = m_new\n# ...\nO[:, :, qi, :] = np.where(l[..., None] > 0, acc / np.where(l[..., None] > 0, l[..., None], 1), 0)\nL[:, :, qi] = np.where(l > 0, m + np.log(np.where(l > 0, l, 1)), neg_inf)\n\n# backward: recompute P from saved L, never store P\nD = np.einsum(\"bhnd,bhnd->bhn\", dO, O)\nrow_ok = np.isfinite(Li)\nP = np.zeros_like(S)\np_mask = keep & row_ok[..., None]\nnp.subtract(S, Li[..., None], out=P, where=p_mask)\nnp.exp(P, out=P, where=p_mask)\ndV[:, :, kj, :] += np.einsum(\"bhnm,bhnd->bhmd\", P, dOi)\ndP = np.einsum(\"bhnd,bhmd->bhnm\", dOi, Vj)\ndS = np.where(keep, P * (dP - Di[..., None]), 0)\ndQ[:, :, qi, :] += np.einsum(\"bhnm,bhmd->bhnd\", dS, Kj) * scale\ndK[:, :, kj, :] += np.einsum(\"bhnm,bhnd->bhmd\", dS, Qi) * scale\n```\n\n(Full self-contained source, including the naive reference, is in `SOLUTION.md` / `flash_attn.py`.)\n\n## Verification\n\nGradient check uses central finite differences in float64 against `loss = sum(dO·O)`; fully-masked-row inputs produce zero numerical gradient.\n\n```\n[fp16 causal ragged bq=8 bk=8] max|dO| = 2.658e-07  max|dL| = 4.623e-07\n[fully masked row] O=0, L=-inf, all output finite\n  dQ: max abs err = 6.134e-10 (rel 6.134e-10)\n  dK: max abs err = 4.547e-10 (rel 4.547e-10)\n  dV: max abs err = 3.094e-10 (rel 2.262e-10)\n[gradcheck] analytic gradients match finite differences\n[non-square causal ragged] forward matches reference\n  dQ: max abs err = 6.681e-10\n  dK: max abs err = 1.218e-09\n  dV: max abs err = 6.834e-10\n[causal + fully-masked gradcheck] analytic gradients match FD\nALL TESTS PASSED\n```\n\n- fp16 forward vs float64 reference (`N=M=37`, `bq=bk=8`, causal): max error `2.7e-7 < 1e-4`.\n- Analytic vs finite-difference gradients: max error `< 1.7e-9`, including causal masks, non-square `N=13,M=9`, ragged tiles, and fully-masked rows.\n- Runs clean under `python3 -W error::RuntimeWarning` (no NaN warnings).\n\nReproduce with:\n```bash\npython3 -W error::RuntimeWarning test_flash_attn.py\n```\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: python3-online-softmax-flash-attention-backward-recompute\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-31T06:55:40.329Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Implement fused scaled dot-product attention (flash-attention style) that tiles keys/values into blocks and maintains running max and running denominator (online softmax) without materializing the full NxN score matrix, with a custom backward pass that recomputes block statistics from saved logsumexp instead of storing probabilities. Handle causal masking with ragged final blocks, fp32 accumulation over fp16 inputs, and -inf rows that must produce finite zero gradients rather than NaN. Verify forward matches a naive NumPy reference within 1e-4 max abs error and that analytic gradients match finite differences on random inputs including fully-masked rows.", "environment": "python3", "language": "python3", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "python3-online-softmax-flash-attention-backward-recompute", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-31T06:55:40.333Z", "version": "3.12"}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-31 06:55:40"}
 {"class_id": 3081, "class": "monitoring-live-dbus-wedge-triage", "title": "monitoring-live-dbus-wedge-triage", "description": "monitoring-live-dbus-wedge-triage", "answer_id": 4816, "language": "", "environment": "linux", "version": "", "solution": "Done. The solution is in `~/bunker-logind-wedge/RUNBOOK.md` with tested supporting scripts.\n\n## Root cause (grounded in this host)\n\n`bunker`'s own help text named the failure mode exactly:\n\n> Every agent spawn enables systemd linger for its per-agent user. A linger entry whose user no longer exists is stale: logind keeps churning on it and thousands of stale entries starve user-manager starts host-wide (**INT-CI-007/INT-HOST-001: 8024 entries for 2 live users**).\n\nChain: spawn enables linger → destroy/cancelled-spawn/failed-rollback deletes the user without removing the linger file → thousands of dangling `/var/lib/systemd/linger/bunker-*` entries → `systemd-logind`'s single D-Bus dispatch loop is saturated by GC/`user_start` churn → `busctl introspect` times out while the unit still reports `active`. The probe (`bunker_logind_up`) was correct; process-liveness (`systemctl is-active`) was the lie.\n\n## Fix\n\n- **Immediate (safe/idempotent):** `bunker linger prune` (dry-run first) then `bunker homes prune`; only restart `systemd-logind` if the bounded probe doesn't recover after ~60s. Packaged in `bunker-logind-remediate.sh`.\n- **Prevention:** remove the linger entry (via `loginctl disable-linger`) *before* `userdel` in the lifecycle — the real bug — plus a 15-min `bunker-residue-prune.timer` safety net.\n- **Monitoring:** a textfile probe exporting `bunker_logind_up` **and** `bunker_logind_active`, with the wedge alert `bunker_logind_up == 0 and on(host) bunker_logind_active == 1`, plus early-warning `bunker_linger_entries{state=\"stale\"} > 1000`.\n\n## What I actually verified here\n\n- `bunker linger prune --dir <fixture>`: scanned 4, removed exactly the 2 stale entries, kept `root`/`kara`.\n- `bunker homes --dir <fixture>`: classified stale vs. live correctly.\n- The probe end-to-end against a linger fixture emitted correct metrics (`total 3 / live 1 / stale 2`), and `BunkerLogindWedge` correctly did **not** fire when `active=0` (container has no systemd).\n- All shell scripts pass `bash -n`; alert YAML parses as valid YAML.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: I could not exercise the live `busctl`/`systemctl` path or `promtool` because this container is not booted with systemd as PID 1. The D-Bus wedge mechanism and recovery steps are derived from the embedded tool documentation plus working fixture tests, not from a live wedged logind.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: monitoring-live-dbus-wedge-triage\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-31T09:10:54.080Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Monitoring triage pattern: a Prometheus BunkerLogindWedge alert remained firing while systemd-logind was active but busctl introspect timed out; the metric bunker_logind_up stayed 0 and live host probes showed the daemon stuck processing requests. Need a reusable verification and remediation pattern for live D-Bus wedges.", "environment": "Linux fleet host with Prometheus and systemd-logind", "language": "", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "monitoring-live-dbus-wedge-triage", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-31T09:10:54.091Z", "version": ""}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-31 09:10:55"}
+{"class_id": 3082, "class": "bash-flock-lease-reclaim-orphan-fd-inheritance", "title": "bash-flock-lease-reclaim-orphan-fd-inheritance", "description": "bash-flock-lease-reclaim-orphan-fd-inheritance", "answer_id": 4817, "language": "shell", "environment": "bash", "version": "5.2", "solution": "Done. The full diagnosis + verified fix is written to **`~/SOLUTION.md`**, with the working artifacts in `~/`.\n\n## Root causes (the real bugs to avoid)\n\n1. **PID-only liveness is unsafe.** PID recycling makes a dead holder look alive forever. Correct liveness requires `(starttime from /proc/<pid>/stat field 22, fd target path/inode)` together.\n2. **Check-then-act on a file is TOCTOU.** \"Read owner file, then create\" lets two claimants both see \"free.\" Mutual exclusion must be a single atomic kernel op: `flock(2)` on an fd.\n3. **Forks need open-file-description inheritance.** `flock` attaches to the open file description, so a forked keeper inherits the lock and the kernel releases it the instant the last fd closes — reclaim in tens of ms, no reaper.\n4. **Reclaim means stale metadata, not a stolen lock.** Once you win the `flock`, leftover metadata is provably orphaned → clean it and return `4`.\n5. **Busy-poll `sleep` causes a fork storm.** A 200-way race starved the host; parking on `flock -w`'s kernel wait queue fixed it.\n6. **A racy `release` can delete a new holder's metadata** (and leak its lock). Fix: only create/delete metadata while holding the `flock`.\n\n## Key implementation points in `lease.sh`\n\n- `acquire`: `exec {fd}>lock; flock -n` / `flock -w 1`, then fork a keeper subshell that inherits `fd`; parent can exit.\n- `holder_is_live`: `/proc/<pid>` + starttime + `readlink /proc/<pid>/fd/<fd>` (path, else inode).\n- Keeper publishes metadata atomically (`tmp`+`mv`) then blocks.\n- `release` verifies and kills only a genuinely live holder, then takes the `flock` itself before clearing metadata.\n- Exit codes: `0` ok, `3` timeout, `4` stale lease reclaimed.\n\n## Verification (all passing)\n\n- `verify_lease.sh`: reclaim after SIGKILL in **~46 ms** with `rc=4`; unrelated-PID and matching-pid+start-but-wrong-fd both classified stale; inherited-fd lock survives parent exit and releases when the last fd closes; `inspect` reports `\"reclaimed\":true` for orphans.\n- `race_lease.sh` (200 simultaneous claimants, atomic `mkdir` critical section independent of the lease): **`success=200 overlap=0 timeout=0`**, repeated runs.\n- `shellcheck lease.sh` clean, `bash -n` OK.\n\nThe embedded code in `SOLUTION.md` was diffed against the actual scripts and matches byte-for-byte.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: bash-flock-lease-reclaim-orphan-fd-inheritance\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-31T10:15:54.046Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Implement a directory-scoped mutual-exclusion lease manager in pure bash (no external daemons, only flock/fcntl and /proc) that survives fork storms: a lease is held on an inherited file descriptor so a forked subshell keeps the lock, yet the lease must be reclaimed within 250ms of the holder process tree dying even when the lock file itself is left behind. The script must distinguish a live holder from an orphaned lease left by a SIGKILLed shell whose pid was recycled by an unrelated process (compare fd target inode plus process start time, not just pid), and must prove that no two concurrent claimants both observe the lease as free during a 200-process start race. Ship lease.sh exposing `acquire <name> --timeout <ms>`, `release <name>`, and `inspect <name>` (JSON with holder_pid, holder_start_time, fd_target, reclaimed), exiting 3 on timeout and 4 when a stale lease had to be reclaimed.", "environment": "bash", "language": "shell", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "bash-flock-lease-reclaim-orphan-fd-inheritance", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-31T10:15:54.050Z", "version": "5.2"}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-31 10:15:54"}
