+<h2>Root causes (the real bugs to avoid)</h2>
+<ol>
+<li><strong>PID-only liveness is unsafe.</strong> PID recycling makes a dead holder look alive forever. Correct liveness requires <code>(starttime from /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/stat field 22, fd target path/inode)</code> together.</li>
+<li><strong>Check-then-act on a file is TOCTOU.</strong> "Read owner file, then create" lets two claimants both see "free." Mutual exclusion must be a single atomic kernel op: <code>flock(2)</code> on an fd.</li>
+<li><strong>Forks need open-file-description inheritance.</strong> <code>flock</code> attaches to the open file description, so a forked keeper inherits the lock and the kernel releases it the instant the last fd closes — reclaim in tens of ms, no reaper.</li>
+<li><strong>Reclaim means stale metadata, not a stolen lock.</strong> Once you win the <code>flock</code>, leftover metadata is provably orphaned → clean it and return <code>4</code>.</li>
+<li><strong>Busy-poll <code>sleep</code> causes a fork storm.</strong> A 200-way race starved the host; parking on <code>flock -w</code>'s kernel wait queue fixed it.</li>
+<li><strong>A racy <code>release</code> can delete a new holder's metadata</strong> (and leak its lock). Fix: only create/delete metadata while holding the <code>flock</code>.</li>
+</ol>
+<h2>Key implementation points in <code>lease.sh</code></h2>
+<ul>
+<li><code>acquire</code>: <code>exec {fd}&gt;lock; flock -n</code> / <code>flock -w 1</code>, then fork a keeper subshell that inherits <code>fd</code>; parent can exit.</li>
+<li><code>holder_is_live</code>: <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;</code> + starttime + <code>readlink /proc/&lt;pid&gt;/fd/&lt;fd&gt;</code> (path, else inode).</li>
+<li>Keeper publishes metadata atomically (<code>tmp</code>+<code>mv</code>) then blocks.</li>
+<li><code>release</code> verifies and kills only a genuinely live holder, then takes the <code>flock</code> itself before clearing metadata.</li>
+<li>Exit codes: <code>0</code> ok, <code>3</code> timeout, <code>4</code> stale lease reclaimed.</li>
+</ul>
+<h2>Verification (all passing)</h2>
+<ul>
+<li><code>verify_lease.sh</code>: reclaim after SIGKILL in <strong>~46 ms</strong> with <code>rc=4</code>; unrelated-PID and matching-pid+start-but-wrong-fd both classified stale; inherited-fd lock survives parent exit and releases when the last fd closes; <code>inspect</code> reports <code>"reclaimed":true</code> for orphans.</li>
+<li><code>race_lease.sh</code> (200 simultaneous claimants, atomic <code>mkdir</code> critical section independent of the lease): <strong><code>success=200 overlap=0 timeout=0</code></strong>, repeated runs.</li>
